[# docs-src/repos/cx-platform-mail.cxd — the mail repository's page,
   repo-cx-platform-mail.html (RULED: DOCS-41, DOCS-47, DOCS-49 wave 3).
   Facts from the registries; the one example from its own corpus (the sasl
   suite). #]

[repo name=cx-platform-mail
  [intro
    """Both halves of mail, each in one call. `cx-platform-mail`
       is SMTP and IMAP as a CX program meets them: a submission client that
       dials, greets, upgrades, authenticates, sends and quits in one verb, a
       mailbox client that connects and reads by UID in one verb, the two
       server cores a CX process serves either protocol from, and the SASL
       registry they share — byte-pinned to the RFC vectors."""]
  [body
    """The common way a mail client is broken is a step its author forgot, so
       `submit` and `connect` have no steps to forget: there is no separate
       STARTTLS verb, no login verb, no ENABLE verb. Partial acceptance is a
       value — `submit` answers a row per recipient — and the IMAP surface
       is UID-only, because a sequence number renumbers on every EXPUNGE and
       a client that computed one is a client about to act on the wrong
       message."""]
  [body
    """The server cores decide meaning and open nothing, which is what lets
       the corpus grade them without a socket: the message terminator and
       its near-misses, dot-stuffing and its inverse, the RFC 5321 minimums
       for line, path and recipient counts, size claims that overflow, AUTH
       refused on a cleartext connection under a TLS policy. `sasl` carries
       the mechanism registry for both: PLAIN, OAUTHBEARER and XOAUTH2
       emission pinned to the published vectors, the GS2 refusals, and a
       downgrade — a token offered to a password-only server, or the reverse
       — refused before a credential is sent."""]
  [gives
    """Three modules: the two protocols, in the platform group, and `sasl`, a
       Ring 1 module that keeps its `cx-stdlib` namespace because a program
       imports it the same way whichever repository carries it. Each links to
       the reference page projected from the module's own documentation."""]
  [try
    """The first exchange of RFC 4616 §4, derived by hand in the case and
       graded byte for byte: the PLAIN initial response for `tim`, as the
       wire carries it and as the decoded octets, the two NULs where the
       standard puts them."""]
  [fixture id=sasl-001-plain-rfc4616-tim-vector]
  [status
    """The mail repository builds on the wire and is built on by the
       application host; the front door compiles its V module from the pin,
       and the module's own initialisation registers the two pure server
       cores with the language, so the core names no product."""]]
